===Etymology 1===
{{wikipedia|Dental floss}}
[[Image:Spinning candy floss.jpg|thumb|right|A woman spinning candy floss.]]

1750, from {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|floche||tuft of wool}}, from {{m|fr|floc}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|flosche||down, velvet}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|floccus||piece of wool}}, probably from {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*flokko||down, wool, flock}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*flukkōn-}}, {{m|gem-pro|*flukkan-}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fluksōn-||down, flock}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*plAwək-||hair, fibres, tuft}}. Cognate with {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|flocko||down}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{m|dum|vlocke||flock}}, {{etyl|no|-}} dialectal {{m|no|flugsa||snowflake}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|flos||plush}} (tr=17c.). Related to [[fleece]].<!-- ?? -->

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# a [[thread]], used to clean the area between the [[teeth]]
# (raw) [[silk]] fibres
# the fibres covering a [[corn]] [[cob]]
# Any thread-like material having parallel [[strands]] that are not [[spun]] or [[wound]] around each other.
#: ''embroidery '''floss'''''
# {{label|en|British}} Spun sugar or [[cotton candy]], especially in the phrase "candy floss".
# A [[body]] [[feather]] of an [[ostrich]].
#: '''''Flosses''' are soft, and grey from the female and black from the male.''

